research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-429-## Remarks
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-430-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-431-- **The two halves are independent.** The derivative clause needs no integral and
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-432-  the integral clause needs no derivative; they are collected in one item because
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-433-  they are the two constructions of the one-dimensional theory that transfer to
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-434-  $\mathbb{R}^{m}$ by the same move, and because
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md:435:  [[cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound]] is what joins them.
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-436-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-437-- **Why the intrinsic derivative is stated first.** The componentwise formula is
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-438-  the one used in computations, but it is tied to the standard basis, whereas the
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-439-  limit of the difference quotient is not. The intrinsic form is the one that
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-440-  survives when the domain is enlarged from an interval to a subset of
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-def-vector-valued-derivative-and-integral.md-441-  $\mathbb{R}^{n}$, which is a later page of this track.
--
