research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a4r2.log-48142- 
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a4r2.log-48143--- **The case $n = 1$ and the published one-dimensional theorem.** $\mathbb{R}^{1}$ is the set of functions $1 \to \mathbb{R}$ and is therefore **not literally** $\mathbb{R}$. The map $\theta : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}^{1}$ sending $t$ to the function with value $t$ at $0$ is a bijection, and $d_2(\theta(s),\theta(t)) = \sqrt{(s-t)^{2}} = |s-t|$ by [L8], so $\theta$ is an isometric bijection onto $\mathbb{R}^{1}$ ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]). Under that identification this corollary at $n = 1$ and the published [[thm-bolzano-weierstrass]] are the same statement, and neither is used to prove the other: the published theorem is proved on the real line, and the corollary above is proved from Heine-Borel in $\mathbb{R}^{n}$.
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a4r2.log-48144-+- **The case $n = 1$ and the published one-dimensional theorem.** $\mathbb{R}^{1}$ is the set of functions $1 \to \mathbb{R}$ and is therefore **not literally** $\mathbb{R}$. The map $\theta : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}^{1}$ sending $t$ to the function with value $t$ at $0$ is a bijection, and $d_2(\theta(s),\theta(t)) = \sqrt{(s-t)^{2}} = |s-t|$ ([[thm-of-square-roots]], [[lem-of-abs-value]]), so $\theta$ is an isometric bijection onto $\mathbb{R}^{1}$ ([[def-isometry-and-metric-embedding]]). Under that identification this corollary at $n = 1$ and the published [[thm-bolzano-weierstrass]] are the same statement, and neither is used to prove the other: the published theorem is proved on the real line, and the corollary above is proved from Heine-Borel in $\mathbb{R}^{n}$.
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a4r2.log-48145- 
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a4r2.log-48146- - **Boundedness of the sequence is boundedness of its range**, a set, and not a condition on each coordinate separately. The two do agree here, by step 3.1 in one direction and by [[thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness]] in the other; what does **not** follow from bounded coordinates is convergence, and the companion page carries that false statement.
