research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2095-## Remarks
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2096-
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2097-- **The two halves are independent.** The derivative clause needs no integral and
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2098-  the integral clause needs no derivative; they are collected in one item because
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2099-  they are the two constructions of the one-dimensional theory that transfer to
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2100-  $\mathbb{R}^{m}$ by the same move, and because
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md:2101:  [[cor-vector-valued-ftc-and-lipschitz-bound]] is what joins them.
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2102-
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2103-- **Why the intrinsic derivative is stated first.** The componentwise formula is
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2104-  the one used in computations, but it is tied to the standard basis, whereas the
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2105-  limit of the difference quotient is not. The intrinsic form is the one that
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2106-  survives when the domain is enlarged from an interval to a subset of
research/audit/wave12-refuter-tasks/rem-rn-conventions-and-scope.md-2107-  $\mathbb{R}^{n}$, which is a later page of this track.
--
